## Founding & History
Eastford Creek Vineyard was established in 2018 on the Nichols family farm. The land, located in Sassafras, Tasmania, was previously used for growing crops such as broccoli, potatoes, and poppies. Farm owners Rob and Sue Nichols, along with their team and family, planted the initial 4 hectares of vines. Between 2019 and 2023, an additional 7 hectares were planted, bringing the total area under vine to nearly 12 hectares. The vineyard is named after a water system on the property, which an old chart marked as "East Ford"; the name "Eastford Creek" was coined when the words were unintentionally combined and "creek" added. The initial vineyard area was noted as being steep, rocky, and boggy, making it suitable for vines.

## People
The vineyard is owned and operated by Rob and Sue Nichols. Andrew Gaman serves as the principal winemaker, having moved to Tasmania from the Margaret River wine region. Gaman is an award-winning winemaker and wine judge who oversees the entire grape-to-bottle process on-site. Rachel and Nick Turner manage the cellar door and events, with Leo Turner working as an apprentice.

## Vineyard & Site
Eastford Creek Vineyard has nearly 12 hectares under vine. The first 4 hectares were planted in 2018, with an additional 7 hectares added between 2019 and 2023. The vineyard is situated on a rocky outcrop, offering views across Eastford Creek and the fertile agricultural land of the North West Coast. Varietals planted include Pinot Gris, Pinot Noir, Rosé, Sparkling Rosé, Chardonnay, Sparkling White, and Gamay. The owners have invested in re-wilding the property, which involves restoring habitat and encouraging biodiversity to allow the land to recover its natural rhythm.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Eastford Creek Vineyard operates from 11 am to 5 pm, Wednesday through Sunday. It is closed on Christmas Day, Boxing Day, and Good Friday, with hours potentially expanding during summer. The cellar door is housed within lovingly restored agricultural sheds, including a Granary and Pumpkin Shed, some of which are 140 years old. Visitors can enjoy wine tasting, platters, and coffee, and relax in the landscaped gardens with views down the valley. The atmosphere is designed to be friendly and relaxed, accommodating both serious wine tasters and families. Both indoor and outdoor seating options are available. The vineyard also participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to ship purchased wines home.

## Food & Hospitality
Eastford Creek Vineyard offers gourmet platters that feature local cheeses, meats, and seasonal produce. Coffee is also available. Lunch is served at the venue. The converted pumpkin shed serves as a space for weddings and other events.

## Awards & Recognition
Andrew Gaman, the principal winemaker, is described as an "award-winning winemaker and wine judge". No specific awards or critic ratings for the vineyard itself were found in public sources.

## Sustainability & Certifications
The vineyard demonstrates a commitment to sustainability. Owners Rob and Sue Nichols have invested in re-wilding the property, which includes restoring natural habitats, encouraging biodiversity, and allowing the land to recover its natural rhythm. The vineyard is noted for producing "Sustainably Produced Wine" and offers "Natural Wine" and "Vegan or Vegetarian Wine" options.

## Visiting Practical Info
The address for Eastford Creek Vineyard is 269 Chapel Road, Sassafras, Tasmania, 7307. The contact phone number is 0455 500 344. The official website is http://www.eastfordcreek.com.au/. Email inquiries can be sent to cellardoor@eastfordcreek.com.au. The vineyard is located on the North West Coast of Tasmania, minutes off the highway, and approximately five minutes' drive from Latrobe. It is situated in Tasmania's Devonport region. Facilities include free Wi-Fi, alfresco/outdoor dining, a bar, carpark, coach parking, family-friendly amenities, lawns/gardens, live music, a shop/gift shop, and is suitable for functions. The venue is pet-friendly (enquire) and offers takeaway options. It is wheelchair accessible and caters to people with allergies and intolerances.

## Notable / Distinctive
Eastford Creek Vineyard was established on land that previously grew market garden crops like broccoli, potatoes, and poppies. The name "Eastford Creek" originated from an old map marking "East Ford," with the current name being an unintentional combination. The cellar door and event spaces are housed in restored agricultural sheds, some of which are 140 years old, connecting the winery to the region's heritage. The vineyard is one of a few in Tasmania that grows alternative varieties, such as Gamay. The owners are actively involved in re-wilding the property, focusing on habitat restoration and biodiversity. Winemaker Andrew Gaman also produces wine for Meander Valley Vineyard. The vineyard participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, offering a convenient way for visitors to transport their wine purchases.

